<ecore:EPackage xmi:version="2.0" xmlns:xmi="http://www.omg.org/XMI" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xmlns:ecore="http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/Ecore" name="tabular"
    nsURI="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:xml-analysis:rowset:tabular" nsPrefix="rst">
  <eAnnotations source="http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/GenModel">
    <details key="documentation" value="The tabular schema rowsets, from [MS-SSAS-T]. One EClass per TMSCHEMA_* rowset, spelled out in full, from that specification's column table and the XSD it embeds beside it.&#xA;&#xA;A separate package from the multidimensional rowsets because it describes a different thing: MDSCHEMA_* describes cubes, dimensions, hierarchies and levels; TMSCHEMA_* describes tables, columns, relationships and DAX measures. A server answers one family or the other, according to the mode it runs in.&#xA;&#xA;On the wire both families are the same namespace, urn:schemas-microsoft-com:xml-analysis:rowset. Two EPackages cannot share an nsURI, so this one has its own and every feature carries the wire namespace LITERALLY. Writing ##targetNamespace here would resolve against this package's nsURI instead, and a reader would then look for elements in a namespace no server ever writes - finding nothing, and saying nothing.&#xA;&#xA;Two sources, and each row says which one it came from. The thirty-two rowsets marked source=MS-SSAS-T-20210406 come from the specification. The sixteen marked source=ADOMD-19.114.12 - and the columns added to the older thirty-two under the same mark - come from the client: Microsoft.AnalysisServices.Tabular asks for forty-eight rowsets and reads them in a fixed order, and the specification is behind it.&#xA;&#xA;Three details are not decoration. rowsetName is the name attribute the root element must carry, singular, because AMO correlates the forty-eight answers by POSITION but looks up two of them - Model and Partition - by that name; and an answer sent as a root in the :empty namespace carries no name at all, which throws the whole correlation off rather than yielding one empty table. discoverOrder is that position. minCompatibilityLevel says from which level the client asks for the rowset at all, so a server knows which answers are expected and in what order."/>
    <details key="oSGiCompatible" value="true"/>
    <details key="basePackage" value="org.eclipse.daanse.xmla.model.rowset"/>
    <details key="prefix" value="RowsetTabular"/>
    <details key="fileExtensions" value="xmlarst"/>
    <details key="resource" value="XML"/>
    <details key="literalsInterface" value="true"/>
  </eAnnotations>
